[36m(TaskRunner pid=1936665)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1936665)[0m First, let's find the LCM of 4, 6, and 10.
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1936665)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1936665)[0m - The prime factorization of 4 is \(2^2\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1936665)[0m - The prime factorization of 6 is \(2 \times 3\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1936665)[0m - The prime factorization of 10 is \(2 \times 5\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1936665)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1936665)[0m To find the LCM, we take the highest power of each prime number that appears in the factorizations:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1936665)[0m - The highest power of 2 is \(2^2\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1936665)[0m - The highest power of 3 is \(3\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1936665)[0m - The highest power of 5 is \(5\).
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1936665)[0m 
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1936665)[0m Thus, the LCM is:
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1936665)[0m \[ 2^2 \times 3 \times 5 = 4 \times 3 \times 5 = 60 \]
[36m(TaskRunner pid=1936665)[0m 
